WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.760
- I have been such a fan of this gentleman

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.510
for so many years.

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:07.287
Please welcome the host of

00:00:07.287 --> 00:00:12.287
"A Late Show with Stephen
Colbert," the Stephen Colbert.

00:00:12.451 --> 00:00:15.183
(crowd applauding)

00:00:15.183 --> 00:00:18.630
- Drew, hello, how do
you have an audience?

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:20.640
I don't understand, I'm so
jealous that you actually have

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:21.760
people clapping and applauding.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:22.600
What's going on?

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.410
- Well, it's funny that
you should ask, actually.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:30.260
It's our crew and you
wanna say hi to Stephen?

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.588
(crowd applauding)

00:00:32.588 --> 00:00:35.011
- Would you like to hear my crew?

00:00:35.011 --> 00:00:36.800
(man whooping)

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.645
I got two old guys in here.

00:00:38.645 --> 00:00:41.210
(Drew laughing)

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:44.600
- Well, I wish you could be
here on this set with us.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:45.470
- It looks fantastic.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.310
You're bathed in golden light.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.400
I love your background.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.250
I saw a wide shot, while I was
waiting to come on the show,

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:53.083
I saw a wide shot.

00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:55.390
I love your background, it's very like

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:59.476
very high Hollywood, TV, 1970s.

00:00:59.476 --> 00:01:02.453
It's very Merv, it's
very Johnny, I love it.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:06.820
- I mean, listen, take a look at this.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:11.110
I wanted to show you a
little groovy shot over here.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:12.033
There you go.

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:15.850
- I like it already, that's nice.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.000
- 'Cause six Stephen
Colberts isn't enough.

00:01:20.361 --> 00:01:23.180
(crowd laughing)

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.375
- Can I ask a question?
- Yah.

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:25.240
- Do you mind if I ask a question?

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.850
Are you making papier-mache,

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.250
why do you have so many
newspapers in front of you?

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.260
You look like the mother of a third-grader

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.532
about to make a pinata.

00:01:32.532 --> 00:01:34.410
(Drew laughing)

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.190
- And it's true, we could.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:41.190
Because I just figured, I
think it's, in some contexts

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.180
I always wonder why is
there nothing on that desk?

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.550
Because my desk at home is a complete,

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.920
you-know-what show, and it's--

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:50.830
- Ah?

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:53.633
Of all the detritus and things that I--

00:01:53.633 --> 00:01:57.080
- I don't actually know what,
Drew, I don't know what.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.610
- See, you have something on your desk.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.900
I mean, most desks are--
- I have a bone.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.360
I have a bone with my name carved in it.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.140
This was a bone that I
found in my brother's yard

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:06.973
when I was a child.

00:02:06.973 --> 00:02:08.680
It's a cow bone, I'll have you know.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:10.313
Before anybody sends forensics.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:15.750
I have a cup, I have my
phone, and I have this.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.150
What is this?

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.040
It's a leather coaster.
(Drew laughing)

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.090
That's it.
- I love the way that

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:26.950
you have religion in your show,
because it shows that it's

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.300
a part of who you are and
it's in the stained glass.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.270
And the essence of it is all around.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.313
But, like most things,

00:02:37.076 --> 00:02:42.076
you don't seem to press it upon people.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:45.220
- No.
- There is a statement.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:49.147
You are making a truth, you
are telling without saying,

00:02:49.147 --> 00:02:50.640
"This has to be your truth."

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.530
Am I correct in that?
- Yeah, sure, sure.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:56.090
I mean, I don't purposely
include anything in the show.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.710
I just talk about the things
I know and I care about.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.240
And obviously also my writers,

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.460
and my staff, and my producers.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.260
But yeah, no, I don't want
anybody to be like me.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.150
I don't always want me to be like me,

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.370
so I wouldn't wanna press
that on somebody else.

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:12.670
- Well, that's a good segue, if you will.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:13.852
- That's what I do, Drew.

00:03:13.852 --> 00:03:16.943
- Ah, I love a good segue.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.960
What persona, character
is the most like you

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:26.330
in real life?

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:30.670
Is it Colbert, is it
the man who is hosting

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:32.090
the late night show?

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:34.870
- I guess, no, this is me,
the old guy was kind of like

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:39.870
a confession of my insecurities
and also my sort of,

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.090
I don't know, appetites
anybody may have if they just

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:48.420
gave into their ego completely.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.390
If they just gave into every indulgence,

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.200
like I have to be the
most important person.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.610
I have to be the thing
that everything's about,

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:56.150
I can never be wrong, you have to love me.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:58.720
All of those things are
always kind of nagging at,

00:03:58.720 --> 00:03:59.850
at least my mind.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.610
That's sort of like, it's
like a confession of weakness,

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:03.443
the old character.

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:08.410
All these indulgences of
selfishness that your psyche

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.730
is always asking you to give into.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:14.410
And the great thing about the old show

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:17.700
is that I could piggyback
on his desires and his ego

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.470
and pretend they weren't my own,

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.090
but really it was just a mask
you could wear to confess.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:24.670
And I think people maybe
recognized that in themselves.

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:27.460
And maybe that's why they
didn't consider him a,

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.690
I can't use the word on daytime,

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:34.690
but it's not a nice part of your body.

00:04:36.436 --> 00:04:39.190
(Drew laughing)

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:42.743
- It's a Chris Evans.
(both laughing)

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:49.620
- Yeah, poor Chris
Evans, poor Chris Evans.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.820
So handsome and fit, I
feel so sorry for him.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:55.180
(Drew laughing)

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.140
- Do you miss that?

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.100
And--
- Nope, not at all.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.290
I don't miss that at all.

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:02.930
I like being me, I like being me.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:04.210
This is so much better.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:05.350
A, the old show, I didn't have

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:08.370
the greatest band in television,
Jon Batiste and Stay Human.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:12.140
The old show, I couldn't actually,

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:16.270
sometimes you just gotta come
around, even the little mask

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:18.530
that you wear as the persona
of a host, and come around

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:20.040
and just speak directly to the audience.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.360
Especially when things
are particularly dark.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.210
Like they are right now.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.710
The brightest thing or
the way to fight against

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:28.680
the darkness is to actually
just to speak honestly

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.010
about it and try to
make a human connection.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:33.430
'Cause that's really mostly
what I just wanna do,

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.190
is just have some sort
of sense of community

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:38.383
with my audience.
(upbeat music)

